# Tins::IP::ttl in libtins

In the **libtins** C++ networking library, `Tins::IP::ttl` refers to the **Time To Live (TTL)** field of an IPv4 header.

## What it is

The TTL is an 8-bit field in the IPv4 header that:
- Specifies the **maximum number of hops** (routers) a packet can traverse before being discarded
- Is **decremented by 1** by each router that forwards the packet
- When it reaches **0**, the packet is dropped and an ICMP "Time Exceeded" message is typically sent back to the source
- Helps prevent packets from circulating indefinitely in case of routing loops

## Usage in libtins

### Getter
```cpp
uint8_t ttl() const;
```
Returns the current TTL value of the IP packet.

### Setter
```cpp
void ttl(uint8_t new_ttl);
```
Sets the TTL field.

## Example

```cpp
#include <tins/tins.h>
using namespace Tins;

int main() {
    // Create an IP packet
    IP ip = IP("192.168.1.1", "192.168.1.100");

    // Set the TTL to 64 (a common default)
    ip.ttl(64);

    // Read the TTL value
    std::cout << "TTL: " << (int)ip.ttl() << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## Common default values
- **Linux**: 64
- **Windows**: 128
- **Some routers/Cisco**: 255

The TTL is often used in tools like `traceroute`, which sends packets with incrementally increasing TTL values to discover the path to a destination.

